  card="The Non-Cooperation-Khilafat Movement began in January 1921, and various social groups participated, each with its own aspiration; all responded to the call of Swaraj, but the term meant different things to different people. In the towns the movement started with middle-class participation: thousands of students left government schools and colleges, headmasters and teachers resigned, lawyers gave up legal practice, and the council elections were boycotted in most provinces except Madras, where the Justice Party entered the council to gain some power. Foreign goods were boycotted, liquor shops picketed and foreign cloth burnt; the import of foreign cloth halved between 1921 and 1922. The movement in the cities gradually slowed down because khadi cloth was more expensive than mill cloth and alternative Indian institutions were slow to come up, so students, teachers and lawyers trickled back.",
